  "narrative": "The 1970s was a transformative time for Black and African women's hairstyles and hair culture. As part of the growing natural hair movement, which began in the US during the 1960s, women started to reject chemical treatments and instead celebrate their afro-textured locks. The Afro itself became a symbol of black pride, with its distinctive curl pattern and rounded shape. Cornrows, a traditional African braiding practice, also gained popularity as a way to adorn one's hair in intricate designs. Dreadlocks, too, began to emerge as a hairstyle that valued natural texture over straightened tresses. Meanwhile, protective hairstyles like Bantu knots and box braids became essential for preserving the health of Afro-textured hair, which was prone to breakage due to environmental factors. While the Jheri curl, popularized in the 1980s, is mentioned as a hairstyle that emerged later, we can only speculate on how these styles influenced Black and African women's hairstyles and identity during this decade, acknowledging that documentation from this era is limited.",
